From pkphlam-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org  Thu Sep 19 16:55:47 2013
From: pkphlam-Re5JQEeQqe8AvxtiuMwx3w at public.gmane.org (Patrick Lam)
Date: Thu, 19 Sep 2013 12:55:47 -0400
Subject: Re: Multilevel Propensity Score Matching
In-Reply-To: <CAAc1OX9cAUUSVJtPqc2EffWkHBE4Oznt8cu-rtwqoEK3RUjUGA@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAAc1OX9cAUUSVJtPqc2EffWkHBE4Oznt8cu-rtwqoEK3RUjUGA@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <CAKV6_L9N6LJnxEGM_Z8z8Ppv9inc=+66XGmbyC-tgN+hyKvAvA@mail.gmail.com>

You can just run your multilevel model separately, get out propensity
scores, and then plug those into MatchIt.

On the same issue, I'm not sure whether running a multilevel model is even
useful here.  As the number of observations within each group increases,
your multilevel estimates will probably converge to the simple logit
estimates depending on the specification of your multilevel model.  And
since you're not interested in uncertainty, the standard errors won't
matter.


